summary: Shell command which executes the test

story:
    As a test writer I want to run a single test script in
    multiple ways (e.g. by providing different parameters).

description: |
    This attribute defines how the test is to be executed.
    Allows to parametrize a single test script and in this way
    create virtual test cases.

    If the test is a file, it is expected to be executable.

    If the test is :tmt:story:`/spec/tests/manual`, it points to the
    document describing the manual test case steps in Markdown
    format with defined structure.

    Must be a ``string``. This is a **required** attribute.

    ``Bash`` is used as shell and options ``errexit`` and ``pipefail``
    are applied using ``set -eo pipefail`` to avoid potential errors
    going unnoticed. You may revert this setting by explicitly
    using ``set +eo pipefail``. These options are not applied when
    ``beakerlib`` is used as the :tmt:story:`/spec/tests/framework`.

    Once the test exits, tmt does not wait for processes the test
    left running in the background, e.g. ``perf record ... &`` which
    was never stopped. If they still hold the test output open, tmt
    prints a warning in the test output, and drops what they write
    later than a second after the test exits. tmt does not kill them,
    but on guests connected over ssh the end of the session sends
    them ``SIGHUP``, and they get ``SIGPIPE`` once they write to the
    closed output. The same applies to ``prepare/shell`` scripts.
    Tests with :tmt:story:`/spec/tests/tty` enabled are not affected.

    To keep a process running for later tests, e.g. a server stopped
    by another test, detach it and redirect its output, for example
    ``systemd-run --unit=my-server my-server`` or ``setsid my-server
    >/var/tmp/my-server.log 2>&1 </dev/null &``.

    .. versionadded:: 1.80

example:
  - |
    # Run a script
    test: ./test.sh

  - |
    # Run a script with parameter
    test: ./test.sh --depth 1000

  - |
    # Execute selected tests using pytest
    test: pytest -k performance

  - |
    # Run test using a Makefile target
    test: make run

  - |
    # Define a manual test
    test: manual.md
    manual: true

link:
  - implemented-by: /tmt/base/core.py
  - verified-by: /tests/execute
